From dc9eeffc4a207a795cb230ec2bc27c0202edf028 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luc3as <luc3as@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 16:33:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/25] Survive a refusal instead of amplifying it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three things went wrong together when Vinted challenged an address:

* A refusal at the handshake escaped the poller's own error handler — the handler
  called rotate(), which bootstraps immediately and raises the same BlockedError
  again, outside the try. The task died, the supervisor restarted it fifteen
  seconds later, and every search on the site loaded the homepage four times a
  minute until the address was blocked outright.
* SessionManager.get() had no lock, so searches starting on an empty cache each
  bootstrapped their own session: seven homepage loads with seven random browser
  personas inside one second, from one address.
* Nothing coordinated the searches. One being refused left the others polling at
  full speed, and nothing bounded the address's total request rate.

Now a refusal drops the session without fetching another (discard_blocked), the
backoff closes a site-wide cooldown every search on that site honours
(poll.cooling_down), the first refusal on a site is announced to status
destinations, a per-site request budget bounds the address as a whole (including
homepage loads), one bootstrap runs per site at a time, and searches are
staggered at startup. The watchdog no longer rotates into a cooldown or crashes
when its rotation is refused.

The Docker image now includes the impersonate extra: the plain-Python TLS
fingerprint is the first thing DataDome looks at.
